<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Nongkhlaw State</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Nongkhlaw</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Khasi state whose Syiem, U Tirot Sing, led the Anglo-Khasi War of 1829–1833 against British road-building through his lands.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Hima Nongkhlaw was one of the Khasi chiefdoms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meghalaya">Meghalaya</a> plateau, lying on the northern slopes towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a> valley. Its origins are known from oral tradition, and the register's start date is approximate.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Nongkhlaw was ruled by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syiem">Syiem</a> from a royal clan, chosen with clan elders, with succession through the female line. In the 1820s Syiem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirot-sing">U Tirot Sing</a> agreed to let the British build a road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sylhet">Sylhet</a> through his territory. When the British began treating the hills as their own, his men attacked the post at Nongkhlaw in 1829, killing two British officers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirot-sing">U Tirot Sing</a> waged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war until he surrendered in 1833; he died a prisoner in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhaka">Dhaka</a> in 1835 and is honoured as a hero in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meghalaya">Meghalaya</a>. Nongkhlaw acceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1948 and is now part of Meghalaya.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1500 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Indian princely states</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:89.55%;width:8.92%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nongkhlaw</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>U Tirot Sing</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Khasi traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Khasi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Hima Nongkhlaw</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1724&amp;focus=nongkhlaw-state" aria-label="Nongkhlaw State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6843 1342 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Nongkhlaw State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7364 1602h0M7018 1386h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7163 1598h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7163 1598h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day India. <a href="/atlas?year=1724&amp;focus=nongkhlaw-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
